PROVINCE OF CENTRAL KALIMANTAN

REGIONAL REGULATION OF GUNUNG MAS REGENCY

NUMBER 1 OF 2021

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF GUNUNG MAS,

Considering:

a. that every citizen has the right to survival, growth, and development in a proper manner and to obtain a sense of security that is consistent and systematic as a form of Protection for Women and Children as mandated in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that the Regional Government, families, groups, social organizations and/or other community organizations have responsibility for the implementation of fulfillment, protection, respect, promotion and enforcement of rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. that to provide direction, legal basis and legal certainty in the Protection of Women and Children, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 5 of 2002 concerning the Establishment of Katingan Regency, Seruyan Regency, Sukamara Regency, Lamandau Regency, Gunung Mas Regency, Pulang Pisau Regency, Murung Raya Regency and East Barito Regency in Central Kalimantan Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 18 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4180);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as last amended by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 52 of 2009 concerning Population Development and Family Development (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 161, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5080);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 11 of 2020 concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2020 Number 245, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6573);
8. Government Regulation Number 59 of 2019 concerning the Implementation of Child Protection Coordination (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 160, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6382);
9. Regional Regulation of Gunung Mas Regency Number 8 of 2018 concerning Child-Friendly Regency (Regional Gazette of Gunung Mas Regency of 2018 Number 257, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Gunung Mas Regency Number 257);
10. Regional Regulation of Gunung Mas Regency Number 9 of 2018 concerning Prevention of Marriage at Child Age (Regional Gazette of Gunung Mas Regency of 2018 Number 258, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Gunung Mas Regency Number 2584);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF GUNUNG MAS REGENCY

and

REGENT OF GUNUNG MAS

STIPULATES:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

Article 3

Protection of Women and Children is implemented based on the principles of:
a. responsive to rights;
b. non-discrimination;
c. maintaining confidentiality;
d. empowerment; and
e. participation.

Article 4

The objectives of Protection of Women and Children include:
a. ensuring the fulfillment of rights;
b. providing justice and equality of rights;
c. providing protection and a sense of security; and
d. carrying out empowerment.

Article 5

The scope of Protection of Women and Children includes:
a. Protection of Women;
b. Child Protection;
c. coordination and cooperation;
d. responsibility of the Regional Government;
e. community participation;
f. guidance and supervision;
g. evaluation;
h. reporting; and
i. funding.

Article 6

Protection of Women includes:
a. services;
b. empowerment;
c. prevention; and
d. monitoring.

Article 7

1. The form of Women's Protection services as referred to in Article 6 letter a includes:
a. legal aid;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
e. social reintegration services; and
f. pre-marital guidance services.
2. Pre-marital guidance services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, shall be implemented by every Woman who will enter into marriage.
3. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be conducted to provide understanding and knowledge regarding family resilience, health, psychology, religion, social and culture.
4. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be implemented by the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's empowerment and Child Protection.
5. In implementing guidance as referred to in paragraph (4) the Regional Apparatus may involve:
a. related agencies;
b. religious leaders; and
c. customary leaders.
6. Further provisions regarding the procedures for Women's Protection services shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 11

Child Protection includes:
a. services;
b. fulfillment of Children's rights;
c. prevention; and
d. monitoring.

Article 13

1. Fulfillment of Children's rights as referred to in Article 11 letter b includes:
a. guarantee of civil rights and freedoms;
b. guarantee of family environment and alternative care;
c. guarantee of basic health and welfare;
d. education, leisure time, culture and recreation; and
e. special protection.
2. Special protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, shall be conducted for:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children victims of kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking;
i. children victims of physical and/or psychological Violence;
j. children victims of sexual crimes;
k. children victims of terrorism networks;
1. children with disabilities;
m. children victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. Regulation regarding the fulfillment of Children's rights shall be administered in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

BABN

COORDINATION AND COOPERATION

Article 16

1. The administration of coordination and cooperation on Women's and Children's Protection aims to:
a. improve efforts to fulfill the rights of Women and Children;
b. improve synergistic and harmonious working relationships; and
c. obtain data and information in the administration of Women's and Children's Protection.
2. In order to achieve the objectives as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regional Government shall cooperate with:
a. other Regional Governments;
b. related agencies;
c. non-governmental institutions;
d. universities; and
e. non-governmental organizations.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. socialization or legal counseling lectures;
c. rehabilitation of victims of acts of Violence; and
d. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 17

1. The Regional Government is responsible for the protection of the rights of Women and Children.
2. Responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. education;
b. health;
c. politics;
d. law;
e. economy;
f. culture; and
g. social.
2. The Regional Government's responsibility for the protection of Women's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted by:
a. formulation of gender-responsive Regional policies;
b. institutionalization of gender mainstreaming within the Regional Government;
c. empowerment of Women in the political, legal, economic, cultural and social fields in community organizations at the regency level;
d. strengthening and development of Women's empowerment service provider institutions at the regency level;
e. implementing Prevention efforts against the occurrence of Violence against Women.
3. The Regional Government's responsibility for the protection of Children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted by:
a. formulation of policies, programs, and Regional development activities with a child rights perspective that are set forth in Regional development planning documents;
b. formulation of norms, standard procedures and assessment criteria for the fulfillment and protection of Children's rights that refer to the norms, standards, procedures and criteria established by the government;
c. administration of child-friendly regency shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. facilitation of the implementation of Regional Children's forum;
e. increasing Community participation in the administration and protection of Children's rights;
f. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of Children's fulfillment and Protection programs;
g. guidance of Child Protection institutions;
h. providing special protection for Children; and
i. implementing Prevention efforts against the occurrence of Violence against Children.

Article 21

1. The technical service institution as referred to in Article 20 paragraph (2) letter a shall be implemented by P2TP2A and FPK2PA.
2. In the implementation of Protection of Women and Children, the Regional Government must establish a regional technical implementing unit for Protection of Women and Children.
3. The establishment of the regional technical implementing unit as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation after obtaining written approval from the Governor.
4. In the case of implementation of technical services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish a task force.
5. The task force as referred to in paragraph (4) may also carry out institutional strengthening through cooperation and coordination with:
a. KLA task force;
b. KRA task force;
c. K/DRA task force;
d. schools and/or educational institutions;
e. Children's forum;
f. children's center;
g. community organizations;
h. Women's organizations; and
i. other institutions concerned with the fulfillment of rights and Protection of Children.
6. The task force as referred to in paragraph (4) is a coordinative institution at the regency and sub-district level that coordinates policies, programs and activities to realize sub-districts, child-friendly villages/wards.
7. Further provisions regarding technical services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 25

The forms of services for victims organized by P2TP2A include:
a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services;
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services;
e. social reintegration services; and
f. economic independence services.

Article 30

1. The Community shall be involved in Protection of Women and Children.
2. Community involvement as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of participation through Prevention, services, empowerment, monitoring, supervision and fulfillment of the rights of Women and/or Children.
3. The role of the Community in the implementation of Protection of Women and Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. providing communication and information through socialization and education regarding the rights of Women and Children;
b. providing suggestions and input in the formulation of policies related to Protection of Women and Children;
c. reporting to the competent authorities if violations of the rights of Women and Children occur;
d. playing an active role in the rehabilitation and social reintegration process for Women and Children;
e. establishing family partners at the neighborhood association/community association or village/ward level by the Community;
f. establishing Protection of Women and Children units within community organizations and educational institutions;
g. establishment and management of orphanages;
h. establishment of Child rehabilitation centers;
i. provision of shelters and/or social homes;
j. job skills training; and
k. providing first aid when violations of the rights of Women and Children occur.
4. Further provisions regarding community participation shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.
